&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This article was drafted with AI assistance and reviewed and edited by the F9XR Team. Version numbers, download counts, and maintenance status change over time. Check the official repository before you rely on them.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built these six sites myself and I'm selling them because I don't have time to grow them. Revenue is small or zero, and I say so below. Each sale includes the domain, full source code, the backend (React + Supabase, or Shopify) and 30 days of handover help.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;72fm.com ($900)&lt;/strong&gt;: a browser radio player with 30,000+ live stations, podcasts and radio tools. Premium is one-time PayPal passes; the first one sold in November 2026. About 1,440 human visits a month from search, social and GitHub, rising.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;events.lat ($900)&lt;/strong&gt;: event discovery for Latin America in English, Spanish and Portuguese. 3,671 upcoming events across 7 countries, 500+ articles, organizer promotion slots, PayPal ticket checkout. About 2,000 search and Facebook visits a month. Pre-revenue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;furnifindusa.com ($3,000)&lt;/strong&gt;: a live Shopify furniture store, 38,547 products dropshipped from a US supplier. $2,305 net sales in the last 12 months from 10 orders; $1,999.99 of that was a single order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;loftloom.com ($900)&lt;/strong&gt;: custom React storefront over a 31,728-product catalogue, 197 design guides, 9 sizing calculators. Pre-revenue; checkout runs on FurniFindUSA's store, so best bought together.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;wine.equipment ($900)&lt;/strong&gt;: B2B directory where wineries compare production equipment (156 listings) and request quotes. Pre-revenue, one genuine quote request so far.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;check.tattoo ($900)&lt;/strong&gt;: AI tool that scores a tattoo idea and previews it on the body before booking. $19 report via PayPal, plus an MCP server and a CC BY size chart. Pre-revenue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you'd rather take over a working codebase than start from zero, I'm open to offers on one site or the whole set. Comment or DM me and I'll share analytics.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A business-class trip in my demo produced four facts at the same time:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the saved fields were valid;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the request could be submitted;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the cabin required review;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;nothing had been submitted.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An assistant that compresses those facts into “approved” or “blocked” changes the meaning of the application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I added &lt;code&gt;prepare_trip_review&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;a href="https://github.com/rajudandigam/trip-workbench" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Trip Workbench&lt;/a&gt; so a WebMCP client can bring the saved request and its findings into the React editor without performing the consequential action. The result explicitly includes &lt;code&gt;submitted: false&lt;/code&gt;, and the trip remains a draft.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Make “prepare” a first-class operation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The caller supplies both the open draft identity and the revision it intends to review:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"draftId"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&amp;lt;open-draft-id&amp;gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"expectedRevision"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The native browser trace used a synthetic SFO–JFK request for one traveler in business class. Its captured result included:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"revision"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"policyVersion"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"demo-policy-v1"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"valid"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"canSubmit"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"fieldIssues"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"reviewRequirements"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"code"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"PREMIUM_CABIN"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"message"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Premium cabin requires review."&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"submitted"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"status"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"draft"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This response does not approve a premium cabin, reserve travel, or claim that a human reviewer acted. It reports the demo policy for one saved revision.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That distinction is easier to maintain when review preparation has its own verb. A generic &lt;code&gt;validate&lt;/code&gt; call can return data, but &lt;code&gt;prepare_trip_review&lt;/code&gt; also places the result where the person can inspect it beside the form.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Bind the review to saved state
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The WebMCP adapter parses the arguments and delegates to the same coordinator that owns the editor. That coordinator knows about conditions the server snapshot cannot see:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;unsaved form edits;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a save in flight;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;an earlier mutation with an unknown outcome;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the current session and editor generation;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the exact saved revision visible when preparation began.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Preparation refuses to run when any of those conditions would make the result ambiguous. It also refuses a different draft, a stale revision, or a request that is no longer a draft.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The client stores the result with an explicit binding:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;PreparedReview&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ValidationResult&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;preparedAtRevision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;submitted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Local edits and confirmed mutations clear the panel. The review is not durable truth about the trip; it is a view derived from a particular revision and policy version.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;saved revision 2
       |
       v
validate policy v1 ----&amp;gt; prepared panel
       |                       |
       | edit/save/reopen      | invalidated
       +-----------------------X
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  Verify tool, page, and database
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The recorded run discovered &lt;code&gt;prepare_trip_review&lt;/code&gt; through native WebMCP, executed it, waited for the React panel, and independently queried persisted state.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;tool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;webmcp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;find&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"prepare_trip_review"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Review tool was not discovered"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;tool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;execute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;expectedRevision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;savedRevision&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;AbortSignal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;6_000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;waitForSelector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'[data-testid="prepared-review"]'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The observed surfaces agreed:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Surface&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Observation&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tool&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;revision 2, &lt;code&gt;PREMIUM_CABIN&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;canSubmit: true&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;submitted: false&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Page&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;same revision and finding; still a draft&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Database&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;revision 2, status &lt;code&gt;draft&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Submissions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;zero rows&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A second check typed an unsaved purpose before preparation. The tool returned &lt;code&gt;USER_EDIT_IN_PROGRESS&lt;/code&gt;, protecting the difference between saved data and what the user currently sees.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Recheck after every await
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first implementation checked the context before calling validation. Controlled race tests showed why that is insufficient: an update could start while validation was waiting, or a user could leave and reopen the same draft before the old response returned.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reliability pass at &lt;a href="https://github.com/rajudandigam/trip-workbench/commit/0691767b8d3cecaaf4c71a53006d10f1ed7a8f27" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;code&gt;0691767&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt; now verifies the session generation, editor generation, active operation, pending outcome, draft identity, revision, and dirty state &lt;strong&gt;after&lt;/strong&gt; validation resolves. Stale results are refused instead of populating a new panel.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The important pattern is not specific to travel:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sessionAtStart&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sessionGeneration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;editorAtStart&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;editorGeneration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;validate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;savedRevision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sessionAtStart&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sessionGeneration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;contextMismatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;editorAtStart&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;editorGeneration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;contextMismatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;saving&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pendingOutcome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;refuseStaleResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;savedDraft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;revision&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;savedRevision&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dirty&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;revisionConflict&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;publishPreparedReview&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Any asynchronous result intended for a visible workflow needs a freshness rule. A matching object ID is not enough when the person can leave and reopen that same object.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Keep submission separate
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The prepared panel points to the existing review screen. Manual submission sends the reviewed revision and policy version, and the server checks them again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is deliberately no submit operation in this five-tool interface. That limits this WebMCP surface; it does not prove that a broader browser agent cannot click an ordinary submit button. For real consequential actions, the &lt;a href="https://webmachinelearning.github.io/webmcp/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;current WebMCP specification&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/webmcp/secure-tools" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Chrome security guidance&lt;/a&gt; also define &lt;code&gt;consequentialHint&lt;/code&gt; as a signal for actions such as booking travel or transferring money. A signal supports safer client behavior, but server authorization and confirmation rules remain mandatory.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The most useful result of preparation is a controlled pause: the saved request is visible, the finding is tied to a revision, and the person still owns the next consequential action.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Redux is a state management library for JavaScript apps. When your app grows past a few components, state starts living everywhere — in props, in local variables, in three different places at once — and bugs become hard to find. Redux fixes that by putting all your app's state in one store, and changing it only through clearly defined actions. It is strict, and that strictness is the point.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The core idea has three parts: the store holds the state, actions describe what happened, and reducers decide how the state changes. Here is a tiny counter example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Action&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;increment&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'counter/increment'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Reducer&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;counterReducer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;switch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'counter/increment'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Store&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;createStore&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'redux'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;store&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createStore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;counterReducer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;store&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;dispatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;increment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;store&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// { value: 1 }&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Every change goes through the same path: dispatch an action, the reducer returns new state, the UI updates. Nothing changes state secretly, so when something goes wrong you can trace exactly which action caused it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Redux is worth learning because it teaches you a discipline that outlasts the library itself. Even if you later use Zustand, Context, or a framework's built-in store, the Redux pattern — one source of truth, explicit changes, pure functions — is the mental model those tools are built on. And Redux Toolkit, the modern way to write Redux, removes most of the boilerplate people used to complain about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How to start: in a React project, install &lt;code&gt;@reduxjs/toolkit&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;react-redux&lt;/code&gt;, then build the counter example as a real app with a button that dispatches &lt;code&gt;increment&lt;/code&gt;. Then build something real: a todo list where adding, toggling, and deleting todos are all actions. When you can follow a bug from a button click to the action to the reducer, you understand Redux.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Who is it for? React developers building apps with shared state — shopping carts, dashboards, multi-step forms, anything where three or more components need the same data. If your app is small, plain React state is enough — Redux pays off when state starts getting complicated.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The limits are honest: Redux adds boilerplate and concepts that small apps do not need, and learning it takes a weekend. Do not add it to a simple app just because it is popular — add it when you feel the pain of state living in too many places.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your first step today: build the counter with Redux Toolkit and a button. Click it five times, watch the store update, and open Redux DevTools to see every action in a list. You have just made your app's state visible — and that visibility is the whole point.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ada works as a React developer in a tech firm where they provide solutions for travelers, they have an app where travelers, hikers can look up their places of interest and it shows them high tour points from the position to the tour point relative to their current location.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It happens that Ada built a component that that marks the direction of a point on the Map to another point. She shows it to her fellow workmates in her team and they were like&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;“Wow!! That’s lovely. Can you share it with me?”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;“Of, course” she responds&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now, she tries sharing the component, but she is faced wih a serious problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;“How do I go about this?”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;She has different options:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Copy and Send the component
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ada can copy the components and send them over to her teammates. But that’s not so efficient. If she makes a modification to the components, she will have to re-send the files over again. And her teammates would have to delete the previous files and paste the newly modified ones.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Share with Git and NPM
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Copying and pasting are time-consuming and very tedious. She may try to share the files with NPM. So her teammates can simply use Git to install the components from NPM.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;She will have to create another project for the component, a React library project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;She will initialize another Git repository, install the necessary tools like Rollup, to enable her to compile the component. Build the component and publish the library.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The overhead here is that she is forced to create another repo for the component and add it to her list of repos to maintain. If she comes up with another component her teammates like she will have to create another repo and share the component to NPM.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Share with Bit
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Somehow, she hears about Bit, a cloud platform that enables one to share components out from their project without an extra Git repo(s).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ada smiles because this is what she has been looking for. She looks up the Bit docs and finds out that it is super easy to use it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;She fires up her dev environment, installs the Bit CLI, and initializes a Bit environment in her project.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm i bit-bin -g
bit init
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then she adds the component’s entry point using Bit’s add command. Let’s say the project looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;trv-react-prj
    src/
        - /utils/
            ...
        - /map/
            ...
        - ...
        -index.js
        -App.js
    - package.json
    - bit.json
    - .bitmap
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Bit forces us to use the Single Responsibility Principle, that a component must have only one reason to change and must be responsible for only one thing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Components are divided into container and presentational components. Containers hold the business logic of the project and are not shareable, while the Presentationals hold the UI view of the project. These are shareable meaning they can be used in other projects without touching them or having any reason to change them — that’s what SRP says. So you see, Presentationals are components that obey SRP and are shareable to the Bit cloud platform.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This will force Ada and her teammates to refactor their code to make it suitable for Bit launch.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;trv-react-prj
    src/
        - /utils/
            ...
            -index.js
        - components
            - /map/
                ...
            - clients/
                ...
            - index.js
        - ...
        -index.js
        -App.js
    - package.json
    - bit.json
    - .bitmap
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;See, they have changed their codebase, so every component is in the component folder and inside the folder, the components have a separate folder, an index.js file at the component folder exports them all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ada can now pick out her component, map folder and add it to Bit's tracked files:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;$ bit add src/components/map
tracking 5 new components
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then she imports a React compiler:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;bit import  bit.envs/compilers/react --compiler
the following component environments were installed
- bit.envs/compilers/react@0.0.14
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This compiler is a tool required by Bit to help it build components in isolation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Learn about Medium’s values&lt;br&gt;
Ada will go to bit.dev to create a collection. She names the collection &lt;code&gt;travellers-prj&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now, she will tag her tracked component with a version:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;$ bit tag --version 0.01&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then, she will export the map component to the collection:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;$ bit export @travellers-prj.map&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;She tells her teammates it and gives them a link to the map’s Bit link. They are perplexed by how simple the stuff looks, the Bit playground and how they could import the component to their project using NPM and any other project.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;yarn add @travellers-prj.map
npm i @travellers-prj.map
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;She tells them all about Bit and how it was able to save her:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Copying and sending components to them&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Creating extra Git repos just to share a component.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Her project lead is happy and announces they will use Bit from now on. And also they refactor all their project to Bit’s best practices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Teams that scale code-sharing and code reuse enjoy faster development cycles and simpler maintenance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ada was able to easily share her code with her teammates using Bit. Her teammates can reuse her code in their projects. There will be no need to rewrite codes from scratch, code in a project can used in another.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Bit is designed to help teams scale the sharing and management of components to hundreds and even thousands of components. From that philosophy, it takes care of the components’ entire lifecycle from development to deployment so that your team can share and manage any number of components for any number of developers. This includes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Managing and publishing any number of components from any number of repositories/libraries at any scale with 0 overhead.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ada can create more components and share them without creating repositories for them. Bit is left for the task, it will isolate, build and track the components for her.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Creating discoverability for shared components so that every developer can easily find and choose components&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ada after moving her components to the Bit.dev, she was able to give her teammates the link. And they were awwed by how they were able to play around the component in Bit’s playground before using them. The Bit’s playground provides enhanced discoverability for the components you share. It’s designed to help developers quickly find and choose the components they need through a universal hub with a rich, visual and interactive experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Increasing the adoption of shared components by removing the main barriers and encouraging collaboration.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The components shared on Bit aren’t for the maintainers, Bit enables it to be for the consumers of the components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All this above means you can do more than install a package; you can actually develop components from any project and sync changes between projects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Just like Ada did, she move her map component her teammates liked to Bit. This map component can be developed by her teammates and the changes synced to other projects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This becomes possible because, unlike package managers, Bit manages source code for shared code across different repos. So you can export a component from one repo, import it into another, make changes and sync them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When Ada built her map component using Bit’s bit build, Bi actually built an isolated React library project for her and extra Git repo. In this case, she has nothing to do with it, Bit does everything for her while she continues working on her project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Bit is an awesome tool you can’t just pass by. Ada was finally saved from the stress of sharing her component with her teammates when she stumbled upon Bit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Bit is fast, user-friendly, and powerful. Try it out today, whether working on a team or working solo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thanks !!!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every React app I have ever shipped used React Router. It was never a decision. It was just what you did, like npm install and a src folder and arguing about semicolons in code review.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then TanStack Router started showing up everywhere I looked. I spent a weekend reading both sets of docs side by side, and here is the honest version, no fanboy stuff either way.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Where React Router stands now
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Router v7 landed in late 2024 and merged with Remix, so it is a different animal than the v6 most of us learned. Everything lives in one package now, just &lt;code&gt;react-router&lt;/code&gt;, and there are three modes: declarative (the classic Routes and Route components), data (createBrowserRouter with loaders and actions), and framework (file-based routes with SSR, basically the old Remix life). It is the safe default. Ten years of Stack Overflow answers exist for every problem you will hit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What TanStack Router is actually selling
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Type safety, end to end. That is the pitch, and it is a good one. You define a route tree, and TypeScript knows your params and your search params everywhere. Navigate to a route with the wrong params and you get a red squiggle in your editor instead of a blank page at runtime. It is at version 1.170 as I write this, and it is very actively maintained.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The differences that actually matter
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Type-safe everything.&lt;/strong&gt; TanStack infers params and search params straight from the route tree. React Router v7 added typegen, but it is a codegen step you have to remember to run.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Search params are first-class.&lt;/strong&gt; TanStack has validateSearch, so you can validate query strings with zod and get typed search params out. React Router gives you useSearchParams, which is strings all the way down, and you parse them yourself. (I have written that parsing code more times than I want to admit.)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Data loading.&lt;/strong&gt; Both do loaders. React Router v7 data mode is solid and familiar, and TanStack's loaders cover the same ground.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Code-based or file-based.&lt;/strong&gt; TanStack does both, file-based through a Vite plugin. React Router v7 framework mode is file-based too. No clear winner here anymore.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Ecosystem.&lt;/strong&gt; React Router wins by a mile. Newer does not mean worse, but it does mean fewer answered questions when you get stuck at 1am.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The code tells the story better than I can. Same route, both routers. First React Router v7:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;react-router-example.tsx&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;createBrowserRouter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useParams&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react-router'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;router&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createBrowserRouter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'posts/:postId'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;element&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Post&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;postId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useParams&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// postId is string | undefined, your problem now&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now TanStack Router:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;tanstack-router-example.tsx&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;createRoute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;createRootRoute&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@tanstack/react-router'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rootRoute&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createRootRoute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;postRoute&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createRoute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;getParentRoute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rootRoute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'/posts/$postId'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;component&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;postId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;postRoute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useParams&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// postId is string. The compiler already checked it.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  So which one
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;New TypeScript-heavy SPA and I want the compiler catching my routing mistakes: TanStack. A team that already knows React Router, or I need some obscure integration to already exist: React Router. The honest truth is the router is almost never the thing that makes or breaks an app. Pick the one your team will actually maintain, and move on to the real problems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The best router is the one you stop thinking about.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  So, what have I learnt?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That "default choice" and "best choice" are different things, and it is worth re-checking every couple of years. React Router earned its default status. TanStack Router earned a real look. I have not ripped out a working router for either of them, and neither should you.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally posted on &lt;a href="https://nayemalam.com/blog/tanstack-router-vs-react-router" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;nayemalam.com/blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Signup email is usually treated like a small feature: submit a form, send a message, then show a success state. In a real React product, it becomes a boundary between browser state, an API, a mail provider, and a test inbox. When those concerns leak into each other, the UI gets hard to reason about and the tests become slow or flaky.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I have found it more useful to treat email delivery as a replaceable product dependency. The React screen should know that a verification request was accepted. It should not know if the message went through a provider API, a local fake, or a disposable email account used by an end-to-end test.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That separation makes a signup flow easier to ship, and it gives the team a cleaner place to inspect failures. The same idea works when a team searches for “temp mail so” while setting up a test workflow: the search phrase may vary, but the application still needs a stable contract.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The boundary is part of the product contract
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first useful question is not “which inbox should this test use?” It is “what does the UI need to know after the request?” Usually the answer is small:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the request was accepted&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the user must check an inbox&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a retry is allowed after a defined delay&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a failure can be explained without exposing provider details&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those states belong in the product contract. Provider response codes, polling intervals, message IDs, and cleanup rules belong behind the boundary. Mixing them into a component creates a UI that knows too much and changes too often.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is also where I separate a user-facing error from an engineering diagnostic. “We could not send that email” is useful in the browser. A structured event containing the provider response and correlation ID is useful in logs. Both can exist without making the component parse a provider SDK error.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What to keep inside the email adapter
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An adapter should own the details that are likely to change. In practice, that includes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Creating or accepting the test inbox identity.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Sending the verification request to the backend or provider.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Waiting for a message when an end-to-end test needs it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Checking that the message belongs to the current signup attempt.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Removing or expiring test data when the run finishes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I like to record an attempt ID alongside the inbox address and trigger time. If a retry happens, the second attempt should not accidentally consume the first message. An &lt;a href="https://dev.to/mrdapperx/audit-trails-for-ai-writing-cron-jobs-ha2"&gt;audit trail for automated jobs&lt;/a&gt; is a useful mental model here: every action should leave enough evidence for a teammate to follow the story later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The adapter can also normalize awkward provider behavior. One provider might return an empty list while delivery is pending. Another might return a message with a received timestamp but no parsed link. React should not carry those differences around like a backpack full of rocks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A typed provider interface
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TypeScript is handy here because the boundary becomes visible in the code. The interface should describe the small set of operations the product actually needs:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationRequest&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;attemptId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationResult&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"accepted"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attemptId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"rejected"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;reason&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"rate_limited"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"invalid_email"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;EmailVerificationGateway&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;requestVerification&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The browser-side hook can depend on this interface rather than a vendor client:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useRequestVerification&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;gateway&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;EmailVerificationGateway&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"idle"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sending"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"accepted"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"failed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"idle"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sending"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;gateway&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;requestVerification&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"accepted"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"accepted"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"failed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The test implementation can be tiny. The production implementation can add tracing and provider retries. Neither one needs to change the component's state machine. That is the real win, not the interface itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Testing the signup flow without real inbox noise
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For component tests, use a deterministic gateway that returns &lt;code&gt;accepted&lt;/code&gt; immediately. Test the loading state, disabled submit button, success copy, and retry affordance. These tests should not open an inbox at all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For browser tests, use an isolated inbox per attempt and keep the inbox work in one helper. If your team already has &lt;a href="https://dev.to/ryanlee91/a-react-inbox-adapter-for-reliable-email-tests"&gt;a React inbox adapter&lt;/a&gt;, extend that boundary with attempt ownership instead of teaching each spec new polling tricks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The browser test should read like a product journey:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;signupPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;submit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;signupPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;successMessage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toContainText&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Check your email"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;verification&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;inbox&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;waitForLink&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;attemptId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;subject&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Verify your account"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;goto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;verification&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;accountPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;heading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toContainText&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Welcome"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The provider-specific work stays in &lt;code&gt;inbox.waitForLink&lt;/code&gt;. If a teammate types “tempail mail” during a rushed investigation, the test still has a clear contract to point back to. A small typo in a note should not become a hidden dependency in the suite.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Tradeoffs and a small rollout checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This pattern adds one more file and a little test setup. That cost is worth it when email behavior changes often or when multiple teams share the signup flow. For a tiny prototype, a direct call may be fine for now.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before rolling it out, check these items:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the UI handles accepted, rejected, and unexpected failures&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;every attempt has a unique ID&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;test inboxes cannot read each other's messages&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;polling has a timeout and a useful error&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;logs omit message bodies and private tokens&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;cleanup runs even when the browser test fails&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the boundary boring. Fancy abstractions are not required; clear ownership is. The most common bug is still a boundary that exists in a diagram but not in the code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Questions teams usually ask
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Should component tests mock the full email provider?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. Mock the small gateway interface used by the component. The provider contract gets its own integration coverage, while UI tests stay quick.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Is a disposable email account safe for production signups?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is test data, not an identity policy. Use isolated test accounts for controlled environments and keep production abuse prevention separate from the test adapter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Why not poll the inbox directly from every Playwright spec?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because duplicated polling creates inconsistent timeouts, matching rules, and cleanup. One adapter gives the whole suite the same behavior, even when the wording in a failure report is a bit inconsistant.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal is simple: React owns the user experience, the backend owns the verification decision, and the email adapter owns delivery evidence. Once those responsibilities are explicit, signup tests stop feeling like a lucky chain of network calls and start behaving like a product tool.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A dual-thumb price slider that works with a keyboard, a screen reader and a form, without fetching on every pixel of a drag.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every shop has one: a slider with two thumbs, "$200 – $800", and a product list that updates when you let go. It looks like a ten-minute job. It isn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The usual first attempt is two &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;input type="range"&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; elements stacked on top of each other with some CSS to hide one track. That gets you surprisingly far, and then the details arrive:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The thumbs cross each other, or get stuck when both sit at the same end.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Clicking the track moves the wrong thumb.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The product list refetches forty times during one drag.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A screen reader announces two unlabeled sliders, both reading "500".&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The tooltips overlap into an unreadable smudge when the thumbs are close.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I maintain &lt;a href="https://github.com/Samishan11/react-multi-input-rangeslider" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;react-multi-input-rangeslider&lt;/a&gt;, a small range slider for React that exists because I kept solving those same problems. This post walks through building a real price filter with it, and what each piece is for.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Install
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm install react-multi-input-rangeslider
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It has no runtime dependencies, ships its own TypeScript types, and works with React 16.8 through 19, including server rendering and the Next.js App Router.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The basic filter
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"react"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;MultiRangeSlider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"react-multi-input-rangeslider"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"react-multi-input-rangeslider/style.css"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;PriceFilter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setRange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;800&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MultiRangeSlider&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;step&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;onChange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;setRange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;formatValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`$${n}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;tooltip&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;ariaLabel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Price range"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That is a controlled slider: &lt;code&gt;value&lt;/code&gt; comes from state and &lt;code&gt;onChange&lt;/code&gt; reports the new &lt;code&gt;{ min, max }&lt;/code&gt;. Because it is controlled, a "Reset filters" button is just &lt;code&gt;setRange({ min: 0, max: 1000 })&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Fetch when the user lets go, not while they drag
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;onChange&lt;/code&gt; fires on every movement, which is what you want for updating the label. It is not what you want for hitting your API.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is a second callback, &lt;code&gt;onChangeEnd&lt;/code&gt;, that fires once when a drag, a key press or an assistive-technology adjustment is committed:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MultiRangeSlider&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;step&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;onChange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;setRange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;onChangeEnd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;v&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetchProducts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;minPrice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;v&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;maxPrice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;v&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;ariaLabel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Price range"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;No debounce timer to tune. The label follows the thumb live, and the network request happens once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Format prices properly
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hard-coding &lt;code&gt;$&lt;/code&gt; breaks the moment you sell in another currency. The &lt;code&gt;format&lt;/code&gt; prop takes &lt;code&gt;Intl.NumberFormat&lt;/code&gt; options, and &lt;code&gt;locale&lt;/code&gt; picks the conventions:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MultiRangeSlider&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;step&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;defaultValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;800&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;format&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"currency"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;currency&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"EUR"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;maximumFractionDigits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;locale&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"de-DE"&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;tooltip&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The formatted text is used everywhere a value appears: the values row, the tooltips, and &lt;code&gt;aria-valuetext&lt;/code&gt;, so a screen reader says "200 €" rather than "200".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Keep the thumbs apart
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A range of "$500 – $500" usually returns nothing. &lt;code&gt;minGap&lt;/code&gt; sets the minimum distance between the thumbs:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MultiRangeSlider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;step&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;minGap&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;50&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;defaultValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;800&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;By default a thumb simply stops when it reaches its neighbour. If you would rather have it push the other thumb along, or pass through it, set &lt;code&gt;collision="push"&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;collision="swap"&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Marks and tooltips
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tick marks give people a sense of scale. Pass &lt;code&gt;marks&lt;/code&gt; as &lt;code&gt;true&lt;/code&gt; for four intervals, a number for that many, or an array for custom labels:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MultiRangeSlider&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;step&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;defaultValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;800&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;marks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"$0"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;500&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"$500"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"$1000"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;tooltip&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"always"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;With &lt;code&gt;tooltip="always"&lt;/code&gt;, two tooltips that get close merge into one label ("$480 – $520") instead of overlapping, and they never overflow the ends of the slider.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  It works in a plain form
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not every filter is driven by client state. Give the slider a &lt;code&gt;name&lt;/code&gt; and it submits with the form like any other field. A two-thumb slider submits &lt;code&gt;price-min&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;price-max&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;form&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/products"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MultiRangeSlider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"price"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;step&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;defaultValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;800&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"submit"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Apply&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;form&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That produces &lt;code&gt;/products?price-min=200&amp;amp;price-max=800&lt;/code&gt;, with no JavaScript handler involved.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What you get for accessibility
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the part that is hardest to do by hand, so it is worth spelling out. Each thumb contains a real native range input, which means:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Keyboard:&lt;/strong&gt; arrow keys move one step, Shift + arrow or Page Up / Page Down move a large step, Home and End jump to the limits. Arrow keys follow the text direction in RTL.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Screen readers:&lt;/strong&gt; the slider is a labelled group (&lt;code&gt;ariaLabel&lt;/code&gt;), and the thumbs are named "Minimum value" and "Maximum value" by default. Change them with &lt;code&gt;minAriaLabel&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;maxAriaLabel&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Touch:&lt;/strong&gt; thumbs keep a 24px grab area even at the small size.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;System settings:&lt;/strong&gt; forced-colors mode and reduced motion are respected.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Make it match your design
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are four built-in looks (&lt;code&gt;default&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;minimal&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;soft&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;material&lt;/code&gt;) in three sizes, each with light and dark themes:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MultiRangeSlider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;variant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"material"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"lg"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;theme&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"auto"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;defaultValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;80&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Everything visual is a CSS variable, so brand colours take three lines:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.price-slider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nv"&gt;--rs-range-color&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mh"&gt;#db2777&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nv"&gt;--rs-thumb-size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;28px&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nv"&gt;--rs-track-height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10px&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If you use Tailwind v4, import the layered stylesheet after Tailwind and override individual parts with utilities:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;@import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"tailwindcss"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;@import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"react-multi-input-rangeslider/style.layer.css"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MultiRangeSlider&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;defaultValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;80&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;classNames&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"bg-pink-500"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;thumb&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"size-8 border-pink-500"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;There is also an &lt;code&gt;unstyled&lt;/code&gt; mode if you want to style every part yourself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Not only for prices
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same component handles one thumb or several. &lt;code&gt;RangeSlider&lt;/code&gt; takes the value in whatever shape you pass and reports it back in the same shape:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RangeSlider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"react-multi-input-rangeslider"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;RangeSlider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;defaultValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;40&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;           &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// one thumb: a number&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;RangeSlider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;defaultValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;800&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// two thumbs: an array&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;RangeSlider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;defaultValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;40&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;70&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// three or more&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://samishan11.github.io/react-multi-input-rangeslider/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Live demo and playground&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/Samishan11/react-multi-input-rangeslider" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/react-multi-input-rangeslider" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;npm&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If something behaves oddly in your setup, open an issue. Edge cases in sliders are exactly what I want to hear about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As a software engineer, I love building tools that solve real problems. I noticed that traditional online testing platforms often feel dull and disconnected. I wanted to build something different—a platform where students could compete in real-time, and teachers could easily create secure, private assessments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That’s how King Ice Quiz was born. It is a full-stack web application with a React frontend, a Node.js/Express backend, and real-time chat capabilities powered by Socket.io.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can check out the live app here: king-ice-quiz.vercel.app&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Tech Stack&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For this project, I chose a robust MERN-style stack to ensure scalability and real-time performance:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;· Frontend: React.js, React Router, Context API, Socket.io-client&lt;br&gt;
· Backend: Node.js, Express.js, Socket.io&lt;br&gt;
· Database: MongoDB (with Mongoose)&lt;br&gt;
· Authentication: JWT &amp;amp; bcrypt&lt;br&gt;
· Deployment: Vercel (Frontend) &amp;amp; Render (Backend)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. The Core Feature: Private Quiz Codes&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the main challenges I wanted to solve was security and privacy for teachers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built a system where teachers (Admins) can create a quiz and generate a unique, 8-character Access Code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;· If the quiz is marked "Public," it appears on the browse page for everyone.&lt;br&gt;
· If the quiz is "Private," it is completely hidden. Students can only find it by entering the code on the "Join Quiz" page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On the backend, I generated unique codes using a mix of alphanumeric characters to prevent collisions, ensuring each quiz has a secure, private entry point.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Real-Time Communication with Socket.io&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I didn't want this to just be a solo experience. I integrated Socket.io to create a real-time chat room.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Setting up the WebSocket connection on the backend was interesting. I had to manage user connections, track online status, and broadcast messages to specific rooms (like sending a message only to users in a specific quiz room). This transformed the app from a simple test-taker into a social learning environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Local Scoring &amp;amp; Fallback Logic&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the trickiest parts of building a quiz app is handling the score submission. What happens if the user’s internet drops right before they finish?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I implemented a system where the frontend fetches the quiz with the correct answers hidden, but calculates the score locally as a fallback. If the WebSocket or HTTP request to submit the score to MongoDB fails, the user still sees their result instantly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. Security &amp;amp; Role-Based Access&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Since there are different types of users (Students, Teachers/Admins, and Super Admins), I implemented strict Role-Based Access Control (RBAC).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;· Students can only see their own results.&lt;br&gt;
· Teachers can only see the results of students who took their specific quizzes.&lt;br&gt;
· I used bcrypt to hash passwords and JWT (JSON Web Tokens) to secure API routes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5. The UI/UX Experience&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted the app to feel like a native mobile app. I implemented a PWA (Progressive Web App) manifest, allowing users to "install" the app on their phones. I also built a custom mobile bottom-navigation bar and supported both Dark and Light modes to make the platform accessible and comfortable for all users.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lessons Learned&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building King Ice Quiz taught me a lot about state management, WebSocket connections, and the importance of security in full-stack applications. It was incredibly rewarding to see the transition from a blank folder to a fully deployed, production-ready application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Let's Connect!&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

